while
  the room has players; ticks never overlap — a slow one delays the next).
  Broadcasting every player to every player is N² per tick: fine for a few
  dozen players; past that send only what changed, or only what's near each
  player via `conn.send`.
- Messages are strings on the wire (max 64 KB); `send`/`broadcast` accept
  objects and JSON-stringify them.

### Room rules

- The live site runs the published `server.js`; a preview runs your
  workspace `server.js` in a separate draft room (editors only) — the same
  split as `/api`.
- **The room restarts** when you publish (the draft room: on the next preview
  reload after a save), and occasionally on its own (a redeploy of the
  platform, memory pressure).
  A restart means: in-memory state is gone, every client's socket closes and
  reconnects automatically, the client sees `onreconnect`, and your
  handlers get a fresh `onConnect` for each player. `env.DB` survives.
  Design for it: keep state that must survive in `env.DB`, and let clients
  re-send their setup on `onreconnect`. Do NOT respond by moving hot state
  into the database.
- Finish your work before the handler returns: `await` everything, and don't
  call `conn`/`room` from a `setTimeout` after returning (use `onTick`
  for anything delayed or periodic).
- Handlers interleave at `await`s (two players' messages, or a message and
  a tick), and two messages from the same player can complete out of order
  if the first awaits. Synchronous updates to in-memory state are safe; for
  `env.DB` read-modify-write use one atomic statement (`UPDATE score SET
  points = points + 1 WHERE ...`) rather than read, compute, write.
- Limits: 200 connections; above ~120 messages/second per connection extra
  frames are dropped. Every message is one dispatch into your `server.js`
  on a single-threaded room, which handles on the order of a thousand per
  second — so send inputs at 10–20 Hz and let `onTick` broadcast, rather
  than one message per mousemove pixel.

## Tips

- The server is the authority: validate every input and derive anything
  security-relevant (user identity, high scores, vote counts) on the server,
  never trust values computed on the client.
- Keep responses small and fast; do pagination with `LIMIT`/`OFFSET`.
- For live multiplayer, use the room (above). For slowly-changing shared data
  (leaderboards, feeds), plain `/api` routes + polling are simpler and fine.
- Errors thrown in `server.js` return a 500 with the error message in the
  JSON body — read it when debugging a failing route. Errors thrown in room
  handlers land in the same backend logs, as does a `server.js` that fails
  to load (every join is refused until it is fixed) and any `send` /
  `broadcast` the room refused to deliver (for example over the 64 KB
  limit): if players "can't connect" or a message never arrives, search the
  logs before changing game code.

